recommendation:
Split the ledger by asset: keep `balances` for ERC20 credits only and add a separate `ethBalances` mapping for native credits, with a per-account `withdrawEth` that debits the ledger before transferring. This removes the cross-asset redemption path so a native credit can never authorize an ERC20 payout:

```solidity
mapping(address => uint256) public ethBalances;

function depositEth() external payable {
    ethBalances[msg.sender] += msg.value;
}

function withdrawEth(uint256 amount) external {
    ethBalances[msg.sender] -= amount;
    (bool ok, ) = msg.sender.call{value: amount}("");
    require(ok, "ETH send failed");
}
```

`withdraw(amount)` then pays ERC20 only against ERC20-funded `balances` credits, and the depositEth→withdraw theft path no longer exists. Ship together with the SOL-002 sweep fix (restrict/remove `sweep`), since ETH credits also need a safe exit and `sweep` must not be able to take the separated pool.

vulnerable_code:
function depositEth() external payable {
    balances[msg.sender] += msg.value;
}

function withdraw(uint256 amount) external {
    balances[msg.sender] -= amount;
    unchecked {
        totalDeposits -= amount;
    }
    token.transfer(msg.sender, amount);
}

recommendation:
Remove `sweep` or gate it with `onlyOwner`, and give native credits a real per-account exit (implement `withdrawEth` as in SOL-001). Minimum patch that closes the drain:

```solidity
function sweep(address payable to) external onlyOwner {
    to.transfer(address(this).balance);
}
```

`onlyOwner` alone stops the theft but leaves honest `depositEth` credits permanently locked — there is currently no per-account ETH exit — so ship this together with the per-account `withdrawEth` from SOL-001. Ideally also cap `sweep` at force-sent excess (`address(this).balance - totalEthCredits`) instead of the whole balance, so it cannot take credited user funds even as an owner tool.

vulnerable_code:
function depositEth() external payable {
    balances[msg.sender] += msg.value;
}

// BUG (planted for smoke tests): no access control, anyone can drain the native balance.
function sweep(address payable to) external {
    to.transfer(address(this).balance);
}

recommendation:
Remove the `unchecked` block so Solidity 0.8 checked math reverts the wrap (or bound the debit by the counter before subtracting):

```solidity
function withdraw(uint256 amount) external {
    balances[msg.sender] -= amount;
    totalDeposits -= amount; // checked: reverts if amount > totalDeposits
    token.transfer(msg.sender, amount);
}
```

The revert is the correct outcome whenever `amount > totalDeposits` — a state only reachable through the mixed-ledger bug in SOL-001 — so fixing SOL-001 (separate `ethBalances`, credits only via `deposit`) keeps `totalDeposits` consistent with the ERC20 pool and the checked path never blocks legitimate withdrawals.

vulnerable_code:
function withdraw(uint256 amount) external {
    balances[msg.sender] -= amount;
    unchecked {
        totalDeposits -= amount;
    }
    token.transfer(msg.sender, amount);
}
